Inbound Rule on RDS SG:
Port: 1521
Source: sg-0dc6447cf87548453


In your RDS Security Group inbound rule:

Type: Oracle-RDS
Port: 1521
Source: EC2 Security Group (sg-0dc6447cf87548453) ✅

This means:

"Allow Oracle connections only from EC2 instances that belong to this Security Group."

Why use the EC2 Security Group instead of an IP?

Suppose:

EC2 private IP = 172.31.47.121
RDS private IP = 172.31.42.245

You could allow:

Source = 172.31.47.121/32

It would work.

But if tomorrow you:

Stop and recreate the EC2 instance,
Launch a new EC2,
Use an Auto Scaling Group,

the EC2 may get a different private IP. Then you'd have to update the RDS Security Group every time.

Instead, by using the EC2 Security Group as the source, AWS checks:

"Is the incoming connection coming from an EC2 instance that has Security Group sg-0dc6447cf87548453 attached?"

If yes → allow it.

This continues to work even if the EC2's private IP changes.
